WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.910
- Hi there, I'm Seth Juarez,
and I'm privileged to be here

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.600
with Dr. Yoshua Bengio of the University of Montreal,

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.240
who is also the head of
the Montreal Institute of Learning Algorithms

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.010
and is an expert
in deep learning.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:20.900
He has a textbook out called Deep Learning,
which I could not find anywhere.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.020
It's really in demand apparently.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:27.620
We're here to talk about his research
and the state of the art in artificial intelligence.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:28.950
It's a pleasure to be with you, my friend.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.650
- My pleasure.
- How are you doing?

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:33.240
- Very good.
- So tell me about deep learning.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.470
For those that have been living under a rock,
that haven't really just thought about deep learning,

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.390
for researchers out there
that are getting started in the field,

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.386
tell me about deep learning.

00:00:41.386 --> 00:00:46.490
Well, deep learning is a particular branch
or approach to machine learning.

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:52.190
And of course, machine learning is about getting
computers more intelligent by learning from data.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:56.270
And deep learning is focusing
on learning representations,

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:59.600
and is very much inspired by some of
the things we know about the brain.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.490
- So tell me about like general AI,
there's this thought about general AI.

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:08.270
Is deep learning taking us along the path,
or is it the end goal?

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.610
General AI is certainly an end goal.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:16.190
And deep learning, among other
machine-learning approaches,

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.730
is particularly focused on looking

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:22.290
for a very general purpose
learning procedure.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:27.090
So you know, within machine learning there are
approaches that are more domain-specific,

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.760
and deep learning research tends

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.600
to explore fairly broad principles

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.830
that could be applied
to all kinds of applications,

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:38.570
which is exactly what we need
for general artificial intelligence.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:42.820
- So what makes deep learning,
because when I went to school we studied

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.350
SVMs and decision trees, et cetera.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.400
What makes deep learning different
than those kind of models?

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.430
Because I feel like, it feels different to me.

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:53.850
What is it that makes it different?

00:01:53.850 --> 00:02:01.990
- Yes actually in the early 2000s, even end of the 90s,
I was starting to ask myself,

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.850
what are the advantages neural nets

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.120
and later deep neural nets

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.280
could have against
what were the standard

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.520
at that time,
you know, kernel machines,

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:15.820
SVMs, and so on.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.320
And I had some intuitions about this
and I was able to formalize them

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.000
and eventually write a number of papers

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.480
showing that by having these,

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:27.810
what we call distributed representations,

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:31.680
where say an image
or a sentence is represented

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.470
by a pattern of activation

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:35.990
in one layer of a network,

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:39.540
and by having multiple levels
of these representations,

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.530
we could in fact have
a sort of exponential gain

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.730
in terms of the ability
of the machines

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.340
to generalize well

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.240
and be able to represent information

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.670
in a fairly compact way.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:57.780
So that's something that really comes,
is important when you think about the issue

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.820
of what is called the curse
of dimensionality.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:03.100
When something that's actually
a big issue for kernel machines

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.990
when you try to learn
a very complicated function,

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:08.480
these deep neural nets,

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:09.740
these have the potential

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:14.080
to generalize much better

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.400
if some assumptions
about the world are satisfied.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.080
So there's no magic bullet
in machine learning.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.710
There's this so-called no free lunch theorem.

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.790
- Right.
- And what it says is that,

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.270
OK there's no universal
machine learning,

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.770
but if we make some assumptions
and we are right in those assumptions,

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.430
then we can win big.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:31.570
And deep learning is
making such assumptions.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.460
Well, they seem to work well
for all kinds of tasks that humans are good at.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:41.120
So my hypothesis is that human brains
are also making those assumptions.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.660
And so as a consequence, deep learning tends
to be good at those things that humans are good at.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:50.350
- So one of the things that I've found is that,
and I spoke to you earlier,

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.750
is generally when I was doing work
with decision trees

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:56.590
or SVMs, I was very cognizant
of feature selection.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.600
And you said we don't do that
with deep learning.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.330
Why is that?

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:02.450
- We don't need to.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:06.010
When you do feature selection,
you take a hard decision about,

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:07.240
I take those features

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.840
and I don't want to look
at those features.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.720
And of course there are cases where it's truly
a good assumption about the world

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.890
to assume that there is only a small subset
of features that are relevant.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:19.760
And in that case, feature selection
is obviously the right thing to do.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.820
But in many more realistic settings,
well almost every feature contains

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.280
some information
that you care about.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.200
So you really don't want
to get rid of all of them,

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.400
because each of them could give you
a little bit of a cue

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:31.670
about the right answer.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:36.730
The problem traditionally,
the reason people are doing feature selection is well,

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:39.160
I don't have enough data,
I could be overfitting,

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.180
so by eliminating features
I could generalize better.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.180
But there are other ways
of preventing overfitting,

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:48.540
and deep learning exploits
some of those ways.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:52.840
And it's actually not
completely understood why, but very large networks

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:54.800
that have many more parameters

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:57.150
than you think are necessary,

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.250
actually you can
generalize pretty well.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:03.600
So they are pretty robust to
having more features than necessary.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.900
And of course, you do need to have
enough data to get off the ground.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:15.250
But in general, practitioners
with neural nets tend to keep all the features.

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:18.870
- Is this a reason why
feature-dense problems

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.280
are being used in conjunction
with deep learning a lot?

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.560
For example, like image recognition,
and text and speech?

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:25.480
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:29.340
And in fact, it's an advantage.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:35.140
It's also a computational advantage,
because it doesn't cost much more

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:38.220
to multiply two big matrices

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.260
on a GPU than two small ones.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:42.140
- Right.
- And so you might as well

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:45.060
go and have fairly large
input sizes.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.600
- So we've talked mostly
about the supervised case.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:55.110
I've heard that there is some research being done
around using deep learning for the unsupervised case,

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:57.350
and I'm having a hard time
trying to wrap my brain around

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.440
what that looks like and why it might be interesting.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:00.810
- Right.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:05.790
So let me give you an example
of unsupervised learning,

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:08.110
which humans are very good at.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:15.580
A child, when she is born, doesn't know
much about the physics of our world.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:21.190
She discovers it by interacting with you know
toys and dropping things

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:23.350
and she starts
to understand gravity

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:25.580
and liquids and all kinds of concepts.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:29.220
And her parents don't need to tell her about it.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.520
- Right.
- She figures it out in an unsupervised way.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.110
She doesn't take classes about gravity.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.700
She just observes things around
her and learns how things work.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.800
So that's unsupervised learning.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.320
And it's a particular form
of unsupervised learning,

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.100
because she's not just ,observing
she's also playing with the world.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:51.000
But that's something that we
don't know how to do really well.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:55.250
And even now, even though there's a lot
of research in unsupervised learning

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:56.940
with deep learning,

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:59.470
the biggest successes

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:01.650
in industry have been
with supervised learning.

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:08.160
And we know that there's all of that data
out there that we don't really take advantage of,

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.820
because we need better
unsupervised learning.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:15.060
So we have many reasons to explore that.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:20.020
- So what does that look like?
I mean, again, when I was studying,

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.810
unsupervised learning was more about grouping things

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:25.630
and clustering things
and finding ways things belonged.

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:27.840
But what does that look like in deep learning?

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.810
Because I mean, when I studied neural networks,

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:32.460
it was primarily for you,
there was some kind of objective.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:36.170
If you wanted to learn either classification
or maybe even regression,

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:38.600
if you could do that,
what does that look like?

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:39.600
Are they clusters?

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:40.900
What is that recognition?

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:42.500
- So we don't usually do clusters.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.440
I mean clustering is a form of unsupervised learning,

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:46.350
but it's a form of unsupervised learning

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.800
that throws away a lot of information.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.980
Once you decided that this image
belongs to this cluster category,

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:56.610
you throw away a lot of information
about the image.

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:00.070
So instead, in deep learning, as I was saying,

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:03.320
the focus is on learning
good representations that are very rich.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:07.940
In other words, I keep all of the information
about the input, but transform it in such a way

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:11.350
that it becomes easier
to answer questions.

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:16.890
So if I really understand well the world around me,
like the child I was talking about earlier,

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:20.570
and I figure out you know,
what are the elements of it?

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:23.700
What are the objects and the attributes
that explain what I'm seeing?

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:30.460
Even if I have no task except understanding
the world, I can learn good representations.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:34.030
So in fact, the early days
of deep learning in 2005, 2006,

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:37.660
were about using
unsupervised learning methods

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:39.780
like RBMs and ordering quarters,

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:43.560
to learn good representations
without any supervised task.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:47.070
Just throw a lot of images
and find good representations

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:50.870
that seem to capture things
like edges and small shapes.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:54.810
And once you've done that, you can do it
with unlabeled data

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:57.250
for which you don't know
what the right action should be.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:03.130
You can use those initial representations
as a starting point or as inputs

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:05.490
for a supervised learning classifier.

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:07.800
And that was the beginning.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.210
Now we're much more sophisticated
but the idea is the same.

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:15.450
We can use unsupervised learning
to discover good representations,

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:19.590
and we can also use unsupervised
learning for other things,

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:23.570
like dealing with missing inputs

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:26.610
or actually generating,

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:31.180
for say images given something else,
like text or whatever.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:35.610
So there are lots of nice things
that can be done with unsupervised learning,

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:39.090
which we didn't know how to do 15 years ago.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:43.830
- So let's talk a little bit more
about this generating images from text.

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:44.530
- Yes.

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:47.620
- I mean, that sounds like,
like if I were to talk about this

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:49.580
maybe five years ago
or 10 years ago,

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:51.610
I'd be like wow,
the computer is thinking.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:54.520
What's actually happening when the text
is generating images?

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:56.300
How do you create a model that does that?

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:01.110
So I would say it's thinking,
but in a very primitive way.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.390
I don't think we have machines right now
which understand the world

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:07.190
nearly as well as we do,

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:11.760
but they are able to extract
these representations which,

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:13.880
you know, we talked about factors

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:16.920
and code for these representations

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:20.460
which control different
aspects of the image.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:27.120
So we can learn, for example, with things
called ordering quarters and there are many variants.

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:34.310
A transformation from the image to this representation,
and also from the representation to the image.

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:37.390
So going from the image
to the representation is useful

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:40.520
because we can use the representations
for say classifications.

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:41.100
- Sure.

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.690
- But going from the representations
to the image is also useful,

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:47.030
because then I could say,
if I fix the representation

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:49.020
I could see what kind of image
this corresponds to.

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:53.170
And if I want to generate images,
and I can control like which categories

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:54.930
and which attributes,

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:56.730
so for example voice.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:11:03.180
We have some students who have started this company
where you can generate different voices.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:08.090
So you can make Donald Trump say something,
and you can play with his voice,

00:11:08.090 --> 00:11:13.010
so you can condition the generation

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:14.240
using whatever you want.

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.600
So say the output here is a sequence of sounds,
and that's what you're generating,

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:22.320
but you can control it
with categories here,

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:25.170
like sequences of letters
and words and so on.

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:29.640
- Interesting, because now computers
are starting to become more,

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.260
like for example
there's digital assistants,

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.270
so say I have a digital
assistant named Fred,

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:35.630
and I don't want Fred
to sound like a robot.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:40.760
You can use these representations
to generate real spoken

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:42.730
whatever dialect you want.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.000
That's what we're trying to do.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:50.250
And the game here is that
we can have many controls,

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:53.780
so we can change the voice
like who is speaking,

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:55.930
we can change the emotions,

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:57.250
and of course we can change

00:11:57.250 --> 00:11:59.720
what are the words
that are being said.

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:01.160
And with images, the same thing.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:05.900
You can play with the image,
you can control factors in the image and,

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:09.140
for example, people have been using GANS,

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:12.060
this new approach that we started here
a few years ago,

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:15.690
to do things like allowing people

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:21.930
to just sketch something
about an image,

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.690
and then the system
would figure out something,

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:27.450
a realistic image
that would correspond to that sketch.

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:29.610
- Interesting.
So talk a little bit more about GANS.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.210
It's not something
I've heard about.

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:32.860
It's probably an acronym, I'm guessing.

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:33.950
- Yes.
What does it mean?

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:36.860
And then the other thing that I'm having a hard,
the engineer in me is screaming out,

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:39.460
like well I want to implement
something like this.

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:41.330
What does something like that look like?

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:44.590
How is that model stored or how is that encoded?

00:12:44.590 --> 00:12:50.900
- OK, so GANS, that's indeed an acronym
for generative adversarial networks.

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:57.630
And it's a pretty radical departure
from how we were doing things for decades.

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:01.280
You know, in machine learning,
the standard way of training

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:04.570
has being mostly reduced
to maximum likelihood.

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:06.420
And some variant
of maximum likelihood.

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:09.050
And GANS are really fundamentally different.

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:14.280
So that's exciting, because you know in science
we're looking for new ways of doing things

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:16.420
because maybe this is
going to open new doors.

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:21.560
And while initially our GANS
they don't work that well, but they were exciting

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:24.410
and several researchers
started to try to improve them.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:28.410
And eventually we started having images
generated by those GANS

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:30.670
that were very crisp
and, you know,

00:13:31.670 --> 00:13:34.090
were able to generate texture and details

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:37.430
that were not thought possible before,

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:40.270
at least and in a reasonable future.

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:46.400
So now we have pretty amazing,
almost photo-realistic images

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:48.870
at reasonably low resolution,

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:53.470
like 128 by 128,
coming out of GANS.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:13:55.860
- And that's again, pretty impressive.

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:00.500
It's a huge departure from what
we were doing before, in what way?

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:01.960
What's the huge difference?

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:07.570
- OK, that's a pretty, that would require
a bit of a technical explanation.

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:17.130
But I would say one important difference
is the traditional way of modeling distributions,

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:19.690
because unsupervised learning
is about capturing the distribution

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:23.790
of the data is to write down an equation

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:27.060
for the distribution
function or density function

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:28.980
and this equation
would have parameters.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:32.640
And then the idea of maximum likelihood
is we tweak the parameters

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:37.150
so that the model would produce a value

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:38.420
of the density function

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:39.730
that's larger on the data.

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:41.810
- Sure.

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:44.290
- Here we don't have a density function.

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:46.680
There's no question that gives us
the density, right.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:51.160
It just, instead we say, OK,
well for this application

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:54.750
what we really want to do
is to generate images, say,

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:57.500
and so we're just going
to train a neural net

00:14:57.500 --> 00:14:59.510
that takes in some information,

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:01.280
maybe just pure noise,

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:04.630
and then massages
the information

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:06.950
and outputs nice images.

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:12.640
And we just train it to do that
by training another network

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:18.300
that's going to learn whether
the output of the first network

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:21.430
look like natural
images or not.

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:24.910
And so that second network
is a classifier and it just learns,

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:28.070
does this look like
this was fake images

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:29.390
that were generated by a machine,

00:15:29.390 --> 00:15:32.710
or this was like
real things, real images.

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:36.650
And then the first network
which generates is trying to fool the other guy.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:38.770
So it's like, it's a game
theoretical thing.

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:42.790
It's very different from machine learning,
traditional machine learning,

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:44.100
in many ways and one of them

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:46.770
is that instead of having
one objective function

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:49.510
that you just optimize, you now have two,

00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:53.180
and you have two agents
that are like fighting each other,

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:56.550
so it's really a different way

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:58.160
of thinking about machine learning.

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:00.660
- It's like a Turing test, but one of them
is actually a computer

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:02.210
trying to see if the other one is human.

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:02.940
- Exactly.

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:05.840
One is trying to defeat the other, right.

00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:10.620
And we now have a theory
that helps us understand

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:12.230
why a deeper network

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:14.440
can potentially have
a big advantage

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:16.900
in terms of the generalization ability,

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:18.660
so how it can
perform well

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:21.690
on new data compared
to a shallow network.

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:27.580
And of course, the thing with the classical machine
running like kernel machines is that they're shallow.

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:31.610
Essentially, they are like a neural net
with one hidden layer in terms of structure.

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:37.680
And so the ability to have the depth
turns out to be quite important.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:43.540
- So let's talk about the first
is overfitting, because I mean nowadays

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:45.320
with like CNTK and TensorFlow,

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:48.080
anyone can go out and start
to do these things.

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:50.970
What are some things that you suggest
to prevent overfitting?

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:57.540
- Well, what's interesting is the old way
of dealing with overfitting, for example in statistics,

00:16:57.540 --> 00:17:00.290
is to have a smaller model
with few parameters.

00:17:00.290 --> 00:17:02.620
- Which is the opposite of what
we're doing with deep learning.

00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:03.260
- Exactly.

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:06.920
We are having these big models
that are over-parameterizing things.

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:10.000
In theory, there's enough parameters
to learn everything by heart.

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:13.900
And in fact, if you train them sufficiently,
they will often go to zero training error.

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:18.950
And you know, traditional machine learning wisdom
is oh, you must be overfitting.

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:21.380
- Right.
- But it's not happening that much.

00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:28.690
So there are a number of things that we do,
like injecting noise,

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:35.130
like augmenting the data
by deforming the examples.

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:40.470
And maybe one of the most interesting
that we don't completely understand is the fact

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:41.720
that we used gradient descents,

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:46.070
is actually in itself regularizing

00:17:46.070 --> 00:17:47.930
and preventing
overfitting to some extent.

00:17:47.930 --> 00:17:54.660
- I see.
- And so you can have very large networks

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:57.400
and still get pretty good validation
if you stop early.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:17:59.730
If you don't train to death.

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:01.210
- Right.

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:04.610
- And you use a validation set to decide you know
when to stop training.

00:18:04.610 --> 00:18:06.920
- So just a standard machine learning things.

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.290
- Very simple things, very simple things.

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:12.960
- The other thing you mentioned is that there
is some theory behind the difference

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:14.460
between shallow and deep networks

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:16.070
in that deep networks perform well.

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.160
Can you speak a little bit?

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:19.640
Is there some intuition as to why
the deep networks

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:22.750
mathematically are just intuitively will work
better than shallow networks?

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:25.050
- Yeah, yeah.
So here's the intuition.

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:33.020
What's happening with the deeper network
is a little bit like what is happening

00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:35.750
when you can write a program
with more lines of code.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:37.250
- I see.

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:41.660
So a shallow network would be like a program
with two lines of code.

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:43.800
And so what can you do
with two lines of code?

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:47.340
You can do a memory look up,
which is like a nearest neighbor thing.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:50.570
And in principle you can do
any function this way but, but a memory look up

00:18:50.570 --> 00:18:53.110
isn't very powerful
in terms of generalization.

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:59.500
But now if I allow you to write 20 lines
of code, you can do much more complicated things.

00:18:59.500 --> 00:18:59.890
- I see.

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:04.410
And the part that's important is
that the result of the computation,

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:07.030
the state of the machine
after the fifth line

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:09.540
becomes the input for the machine

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:12.370
computing the sixth line,
and then so on.

00:19:12.370 --> 00:19:18.550
So each layer produces a new representation
which becomes the input,

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:21.740
and sort of features or concepts
that are going to be combined

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:23.240
to build something
a bit more abstract,

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:25.130
a bit more complicated
for the next level, and so on.

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:27.470
And this way you can build
more complicated abstractions.

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:33.290
And what the theory showed us
is that really what it boils down

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:35.720
to is that functions

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:37.910
that look very complicated,
for example,

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:40.450
in the case of that piecewise
linear function,

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:42.860
you can count how many pieces
are there in a function.

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:45.240
So this is kind of a measure
of how complex it looks like.

00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:47.260
It has many pieces.

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:50.140
You think it's a very
complicated function.

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:54.240
And if you were to represent a function
like this with a shallow network or a kernel machine,

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:57.480
you would basically need
one unit per piece, right?

00:19:58.180 --> 00:20:00.780
Say hey, there's this piece,
and then there's this piece and then this piece.

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:05.780
OK, so you just literally, you know,
cut and paste all the pieces together.

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:13.980
But what's happening with deep nets
is that each level kind of falls on itself.

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:20.800
And if you had some number
of pieces at one level,

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:22.360
when you put the next level it

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:23.890
is like you squared
a number of pieces.

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:30.200
You can combine a piece here,
with a piece here,

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:34.090
and now you have
like many more pieces.

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:37.200
And the more you do this,
in fact the number of pieces

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:39.460
you get after say

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:42.790
K layers is exponential
in the number of layers.

00:20:42.790 --> 00:20:46.190
- So you're able to approximate
more complicated functions?

00:20:46.190 --> 00:20:47.680
- Well not all of them.

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:48.670
That's the thing.

00:20:48.670 --> 00:20:54.870
So there are functions
which look very complicated

00:20:54.870 --> 00:20:57.420
that really can be expressed
in a much more compact form.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:59.240
- Sure.
- And these are the functions

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:02.110
where the neural nets,
the deep neural nets are really thriving.

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:08.530
If the pieces were put completely arbitrarily
then we would not be better off than a shallow net

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:10.310
or a kernel machine.

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:17.200
But because there is some kind of structure
that explains the shape of that function,

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:21.740
the network can actually represent
that function with a very,

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:23.200
very small number of parameters.

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:27.080
So we have a function that looks like it
has an exponential complexity, but actually it's not.

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:28.580
It has a small number of parameters.

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:32.410
- So what's new in the field of deep learning?

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:34.770
What are the things that you're excited about?

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:36.290
What's the next challenge?

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:42.550
- Well, I think one of the hottest areas
right now in deep learning is the intersection

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:44.320
between deep learning
and reinforcement learning.

00:21:45.220 --> 00:21:51.390
If you look at the last ICLR conference,
you have lots and lots of papers,

00:21:52.010 --> 00:21:54.090
you know, mixing these two things.

00:21:54.090 --> 00:22:02.200
And so one thing that I'm interested
in is how a learning agent,

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:04.780
which is not just
observing the world

00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:06.090
but is acting in the world,

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:08.700
that's where the reinforcement
learning part becomes important,

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:13.110
can discover good representations
about the world.

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:20.220
And in particular I'm interested
in how the agent can learn to control the world,

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:21.940
to control aspects
of the environment,

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:25.770
and then using that information
at the same time

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:28.180
as it's learning to do that,
to build good representations

00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:31.140
where the different aspects
that it can control corresponds

00:22:31.140 --> 00:22:33.590
to different dimensions
in its representation.

00:22:34.190 --> 00:22:41.400
So I know what I'm saying is a bit abstract,
but basically the idea is, think about babies.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:47.610
So they're not just doing random movements,
they're actually trying to control things around them

00:22:47.610 --> 00:22:49.210
and then control their parents.

00:22:49.910 --> 00:22:52.750
- Of course.
- But while they're doing that,

00:22:52.750 --> 00:22:54.070
this is how they are building

00:22:54.070 --> 00:22:55.830
their mental model
of how the world works,

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:57.290
of the causal relationships

00:22:57.290 --> 00:22:59.290
and how to represent
that in their brain.

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:01.320
So we're trying to do something similar.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:04.810
- Is that similar to the adversarial networks
that you were talking about?

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:07.180
- No.

00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:14.050
But it's related in the sense that we're trying
to learn good representations.

00:23:14.050 --> 00:23:14.510
- I see.

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:20.380
But now, whereas in the traditional GANS
we're just observing a bunch of images or sounds,

00:23:20.380 --> 00:23:23.900
here we allowed the learner
to interact with the world.

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:29.620
So it's difficult because you can't just have now
a big data set and we learn on the data set.

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:31.130
We have to build an environment.

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:36.310
Like think of it like a video game
where the agent can now do things

00:23:36.310 --> 00:23:39.050
and then get some rewards and move around

00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:41.690
and see what the effect
of the actions are and so on.

00:23:41.690 --> 00:23:45.550
- So effectively, you're making
a little baby neural network.

00:23:45.550 --> 00:23:46.030
- That's right.

00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:50.400
- And you're putting it in this
virtual environment, and you're saying do stuff.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:52.860
- Yeah.
- And then you either punish it or reward it.

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:54.050
- Right.
Right.

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:55.540
- And what is it that you're expecting?

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:56.730
What is it going to learn?

00:23:56.730 --> 00:23:58.590
Like representation that are set.

00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:01.460
- It learns how to control
every aspect of its environment.

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:05.890
So then once it's learned that, it can do anything.

00:24:05.890 --> 00:24:12.120
So once you know what the effect of all your actions
will be and how things work in the world,

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:14.680
I can ask you, can you please fetch this,

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:18.510
even though you've never been there,
you know the map, you know the effect of your muscles.

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:20.170
You can you can just plan it and do it.

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:24.340
So once you understand the world,
you can act in it intelligently,

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:27.040
and that's why it's so important

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:28.860
to build machines
that understand the world

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:32.910
and not just compute some kind
of low level statistics

00:24:32.910 --> 00:24:36.940
that give us cues about classification
or something else.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:39.340
- And this to me is,
this is really interesting

00:24:39.340 --> 00:24:42.660
because just the amount of engineering
discipline you would need,

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:44.870
for example just to create
a virtual environment,

00:24:44.870 --> 00:24:47.390
just to create an environment where it learns,

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:49.430
where you can give it feedback,

00:24:49.430 --> 00:24:50.710
is pretty impressive.

00:24:50.710 --> 00:24:53.310
Did you start research on that already,
or is this the next thing?

00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:54.800
- No, we are starting that.

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:55.700
We're not the only ones.

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:59.930
Our friends in other big machine learning labs
are also exploring this kind of thing.

00:24:59.930 --> 00:25:06.210
So I think it's an important direction
and many people are recognizing that.

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:09.120
- So where can we learn more about your work
and what you're doing here?

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:14.110
-Well, besides the book that gives us
the building blocks

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:17.090
and the mathematics to understand deep learning,

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:20.360
there is a lot going on
in the community.

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:23.140
And everything that people
are doing in this community,

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:26.690
fortunately, is available online,
very often on archive.

00:25:26.690 --> 00:25:33.340
A good place to look at the recent papers in
deep learning is the ICLR conference proceedings.

00:25:33.340 --> 00:25:36.560
And there's so much to read.

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:37.610
I can't keep track.

00:25:37.610 --> 00:25:43.400
But you know, I think people should just spend
more time reading and playing with those systems.

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:46.780
There's a lot of software libraries
that people can play with.

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:48.400
That's the right thing to do.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:50.050
- Well thanks so much
for spending some time with us.

00:25:50.050 --> 00:25:52.740
By the way, the book, Deep Learning,
you should check it out.

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:57.170
It has a really rigorous introduction,
because it feels like it's the first soup

00:25:57.170 --> 00:25:58.590
to nuts book on deep learning.

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:02.930
It starts with linear algebra, probably statistics
and then it goes into optimization.

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:05.370
Which is pretty impressive
for a book of that type.

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:08.840
I always thought deep learning, you required
this immense amount of knowledge

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:10.420
before you even got into it.

00:26:10.420 --> 00:26:11.420
And it's cool to see that.

00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:18.480
- So we wrote that book with the idea
that any engineer who has basic math

00:26:18.480 --> 00:26:20.680
and knows how to program should be able
to read the book

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:23.020
and get into deep learning.

00:26:23.020 --> 00:26:27.460
And indeed, by the number of sales,
I guess it's much more

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:29.400
than the number of machine
learning people in the world.

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:31.590
- Yes.
- So that must be that

00:26:31.590 --> 00:26:35.440
some of these engineers are buying the book
and jumping into deep learning.

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:38.860
- Again, I could not find it in Redmond at all,
and I looked very hard.

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:39.900
Thanks so much for being with us.

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:40.460
- My pleasure.

